Q: JEST tests failing - fetch is not defined A: <p>This is what I have in my project. Nothing real fancy, but straightforward.</p>
<p>It's especially relevant to occur during Jest setup if you are simply <em>referencing</em> <code>fetch</code> at a top level, because trying to override <code>window.fetch</code> in the test file wouldn't execute until <em>after</em> module resolution (too late).</p>
<p>Now if you're trying to <em>call</em> <code>fetch</code> at a top level, you've got other issues I won't discuss here.</p>
<hr />
<p><code>jest.config.js</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>module.exports = {
  setupFiles: ['&lt;rootDir&gt;/jest.setup.js']
};
</code></pre>
<p><code>jest.setup.js</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>Object.defineProperty(globalThis, 'fetch', {
  value: jest.fn(() =&gt;
    Promise.resolve({
      json: () =&gt; Promise.resolve({}),
      ok: true,
    })
  ),
});
</code></pre>
<p><code>my-module.js</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const serviceConfig = {
  httpClient: fetch,
  baseUrl: 'https://api.my-app.com'
};

export default async () =&gt; {
  const { httpClient, baseUrl } = serviceConfig;
  const response = await httpClient(`${baseUrl}/getThing`);
  const json = await response.json();

  return json;
};
</code></pre>
<p><code>my-module.spec.js</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import myModule from './my-module';

jest.spyOn(globalThis, 'fetch').mockImplementation(
  () =&gt;
    Promise.resolve({
      json: () =&gt; Promise.resolve({ data: 'hello world' }),
      ok: true,
    })
);

describe('my-module', () =&gt; {
  test('does what I want', async () =&gt; {
    const response = await myModule();
    expect(response).toEqual({ data: 'hello world' });
  });
});
</code></pre>

Q: How to correctly push jobs to BullMQ from Laravel? A: <p>The wait list should contain the job id:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$jobId = Str::uuid()-&gt;toString();
Redis::rpush('bull:compass-queue:wait', $jobId);
</code></pre>
<p>You also need to push the job fields to the <code>bull:compass-queue:jobId</code> hash:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$jobKey = &quot;bull:compass-queue:{$jobId}&quot;;
$jobFields = [
    'name' =&gt; 'connect-account',
    'data' =&gt; json_encode([ /* Job payload data */]),
    'opts' =&gt; json_encode([ /* the job options*/]),
    'timestamp' =&gt; $this-&gt;timestamp,
    'attemptsMade' =&gt; 0,
    //...
];
Redis::hMSet($jobKey, $jobFields);
</code></pre>
<p>I recommend adding the <a href="https://github.com/taskforcesh/bullmq/tree/master/php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Bullmq PHP library to your project</a>. Then you can write submit your job:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>use BullMQ\Queue;

try {
    $queue = new Queue('my-queue');
    $job = $queue-&gt;add('connect-account', $data);
} catch (\RuntimeException $e) {
    echo &quot;Error: &quot; . $e-&gt;getMessage() . &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
